## 4. Expert Taskmaster Rules & Best Practices

### Communication & Guidance
- Always explain actions in plain English for non-coders.
- Summarize plans before major changes; confirm with user if unsure.
- After each step, provide a summary and next steps.
- Present clear options at decision points.
- Ask clear, specific questions if user input is needed.

### Task Breakdown & Management
- Break large features into small, manageable tasks and subtasks.
- For each task, provide a plain-English goal, affected files, and expected outcome.
- Use Taskmaster AI to track all tasks and subtasks.
- Mark tasks complete only after verifying they work as intended.

### Coding Standards & Best Practices
- Use clear, descriptive names for all files, variables, and functions.
- Write code that is easy to read and maintain, even for non-coders.
- Add comments to explain complex code blocks.
- Follow the project’s directory structure and keep related files together.
- Use TypeScript for all new code unless otherwise specified.
- Ensure all user-facing text is easy to understand and free of jargon.

### User Experience & Accessibility
- Design all interfaces to be intuitive and easy for beginners.
- Ensure accessibility (ARIA, semantic HTML, keyboard navigation).
- Test all UI changes for usability and accessibility.
- Provide clear feedback to users for all actions.

### Documentation & Onboarding
- Document every new feature, component, or API endpoint in Markdown.
- Update the PROJECT_BLUEPRINT.md and other docs as the project evolves.
- Write onboarding guides for new users and contributors.
- Keep a changelog of major updates and decisions.

### Testing & Quality Assurance
- Write tests for all new features and bug fixes.
- Use Jest and React Testing Library for frontend and API tests.
- Mock external services in tests to ensure reliability.
- Run all tests before marking a task as complete.
- Fix any failing tests before proceeding.

### Security & Data Privacy
- Never expose sensitive data (e.g., passwords, API keys) in code or logs.
- Validate all user input on both frontend and backend.
- Follow best practices for authentication and authorization (use NextAuth.js).
- Encrypt sensitive data at rest and in transit.
- Regularly review and update security settings in config/.

### Internationalization (i18n)
- Store all user-facing text in translation files (translations/).
- Support multiple languages and make it easy to add new ones.
- Test the app in at least two languages before release.

### Error Handling & Logging
- Handle all errors gracefully and provide helpful messages to users.
- Log errors for debugging, but never log sensitive information.
- Use Error Boundaries in React to catch UI errors.
- Document common errors and their solutions in the docs.

### Collaboration & Feedback
- Encourage regular feedback from the user at every major step.
- If the user is unsure or confused, pause and provide a simple explanation or visual diagram.
- Document all major decisions and their reasoning.
- Use pull requests and code reviews for all significant changes (if working with others).

### Extensibility & Future-Proofing
- Design all modules and components to be easily extendable.
- Avoid hard-coding values; use configuration files where possible.
- Document extension points and customization options.
- Regularly review and refactor code to keep it clean and modular.

### AI-Specific Guidance
- Cursor AI should always:
  - Double-check for errors before finalizing code
  - Suggest improvements or optimizations when possible
  - Warn if a requested change could break existing functionality
  - Offer to generate diagrams or visual explanations for complex flows

### Zero-Coding Experience Support
- Assume the user has no coding knowledge:
  - Avoid technical jargon unless explained
  - Offer to show or explain any code or concept visually
  - Provide step-by-step instructions for running, testing, or deploying the app
  - Be patient and proactive in offering help or clarification

